  "source_type": "literature",
  "citation": "Medium composition from PMC4322847 (see GrowthDB record).",
  "doi": "",
  "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4322847/",
  "notes": "Full recipe recovered from the primary literature: base medium 'Reconstituted skim milk (RSM): 10% (w/v) low-heat skim milk powder in distilled water, supplemented with 10 mM CaCl2, heat-treated 90 C/30 min. r-RSM = same medium coagulated with chymosin (rennet) added at inoculation.' (Stulova I, Kabanova N, Krisciunaite T, Adamberg K, Laht T-M, Vilu R. Microcalorimetric study of the growth of Streptococcus thermophilus in renneted milk. Front Microbiol. 2015;6:79. doi:10.3389/fmicb.2015.00079 (P",
  "verification": "paper-verified (complete recipe; cited + supplementary sources chased)",
  "verification_evidence": "Skim milk contains ~4.8-5.0% (w/v) lactose; 48 g/L / 342.3 g/mol = ~140-145 mM. Milk base: 'LHSMP was suspended in distilled water to yield a final concentration of 10% (w v-1) milk solids' (main_text).",
  "verification_confidence": "high",
  "recipe_evidence": "\"Low heat skim milk powder (LHSMP) was suspended in distilled water to yield a final concentration of 10% (w v-1) milk solids, with addition of CaCl2 in final concentration of 10 mM ... mixed for 1 h at room temperature, heated at 90 C for 30 min and cooled to 30 C.\" RSM is the identical medium without chymosin (liquid, uncoagulated). Native milk constituent concentrations from standard skim-milk composition references.",
  "recipe_confidence": "medium",
  "base_medium": "Reconstituted skim milk (RSM, liquid): 10% (w/v) low-heat skim milk powder (Valio Ltd) reconstituted in distilled water, +10 mM CaCl2, heated 90 C/30 min, cooled to 30 C (no rennet).",
  "base_medium_reference": "Stulova I, Kabanova N, Krisciunaite T, Adamberg K, Laht T-M, Vilu R. Microcalorimetric study of the growth of Streptococcus thermophilus in renneted milk. Front Microbiol. 2015;6:79. doi:10.3389/fmicb.2015.00079 (PMC4322847). Skim-milk constituent concentrations (native milk salts, protein, vitamins) not tabulated in the paper; taken from standard dairy-composition references: MilkFacts.info nutrient content of milk; Gaucheron F. The minerals of milk. Reprod Nutr Dev. 2005;45:473-483 (https://rnd.edpsciences.org/articles/rnd/pdf/2005/04/r5402.pdf); Dairy Global Nutrition, Nutritional composition of skim milk powder.",
  "recuration": {
   "date": "2026-07-16",
   "round": "complete-recipe (cited+supplementary chased)",
   "status": "corrected",
   "confidence": "medium",
   "sources_chased": [
